  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nStrong convection moved from the Capital Region and mid Hudson\nValley eastward into the Taconics and Berkshires during the late\nafternoon hours. Numerous reports of wind damage occurred in \nthese areas. With the main activity now east of the region, some\nisolated convection is still ongoing, but this is generally on\nthe weaker side. Over the next few hours, some cloud-to-ground\nlightning and gusty winds may occur within storms, but severe\nstorms are no longer anticipated. Based on CAMs, activity\nshould diminish by midnight, with just some passing clouds and\npatchy fog for the late night hours. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfternoon Update: No changes. See discussion below for more\ndetails.\n\nA cold front will move through the region Sunday morning, with \ndecreasing humidity levels expected in its wake for Sunday \nafternoon as dewpoints drop into the 60s for most areas, with \neven some 50s possible across portions of the SW Adirondacks and\nMohawk Valley by late afternoon. So, despite similar maximum \ntemperatures to today (85-90 in valleys and upper 70s/lower 80s \nfor higher terrain), max heat indices will be even less, mainly \nin the upper 80s/lower 90s in valley areas. It will also become \nbreezy Sunday afternoon in the wake of the front, with \nwest/northwest wind potentially gusting up to 20-25 mph at \ntimes.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAfternoon Update: No changes. See discussion below for more \ndetails.\n\nWeather pattern becomes more active this week as broad upper\nlevel troughing develops across the Great Lakes/Northeast and \nSE Canada. Strengthening mid/upper level winds (H500 winds 30-50\nKT) on the south/southwest periphery of the developing trough \nwill enhance the potential for stronger thunderstorms with any \ndisturbance that passes through, especially should they track \nfarther north allowing for pockets of greater instability to \ndevelop across our region. One disturbance looks to pass through\nMonday afternoon/evening, then again possibly late \nTuesday/early Wednesday and perhaps again toward late week. Will\nhave to watch trends with each system that passes through for \nthe possibility of strong/severe thunderstorms given the strong \nwind fields aloft.",
